{"name":"Radioactive Eggplant","hex":"#f9006f","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"radioactive","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Exhibiting radioactivity.","Dangerous and disgusting, particularly of people or ideas."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any radioactive substance."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/radioactive"},{"term":"eggplant","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The plant Solanum melongena or its edible fruit; an aubergine.","A dark purple color, like that of the skin of this fruit."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/eggplant"}],"entries":[{"title":"Radioactive decay","blocks":[{"text":"Radioactive decay is the process by which an unstable atomic nucleus loses energy by radiation. A material containing unstable nuclei is considered radioactive. Three of the most common types of decay are alpha, beta, and gamma decay. The weak force is the mechanism that is responsible for beta decay, while the other two are governed by the electromagnetic and nuclear forces."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/NuclearReaction.svg/330px-NuclearReaction.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Radioactive_decay"},{"title":"Eggplant","blocks":[{"text":"Solanum melongena is a plant species in the nightshade family Solanaceae, grown worldwide for its edible fruit, typically used as a vegetable in cooking. It is known by a number of names, including eggplant, aubergine, and brinjal."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Solanum_melongena_24_08_2012_%281%29.JPG/330px-Solanum_melongena_24_08_2012_%281%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eggplant"},{"title":"Eggplant (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Eggplant is a dark purple or brownish-purple color that resembles the color of the outer skin of European eggplants. Another name for the color eggplant is aubergine."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/Aubergine.jpg/330px-Aubergine.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eggplant_(color)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Pierre_and_Marie_Curie.jpg/500px-Pierre_and_Marie_Curie.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Pierre_and_Marie_Curie.jpg/1280px-Pierre_and_Marie_Curie.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Pierre_and_Marie_Curie.jpg/500px-Pierre_and_Marie_Curie.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pierre and Marie Curie in their Paris laboratory, before 1907","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APierre_and_Marie_Curie.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Crookes_tube_xray_experiment.jpg/500px-Crookes_tube_xray_experiment.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Crookes_tube_xray_experiment.jpg/500px-Crookes_tube_xray_experiment.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Taking an X-ray image with early Crookes tube apparatus in 1896. The Crookes tube is visible in the centre. The standing man is viewing his hand with a fluoroscope screen; this was a common way of setting up the tube. No precautions against radiation exposure are being taken; its hazards were not known at the time.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACrookes_tube_xray_experiment.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Radioactivity_and_radiation.png/500px-Radioactivity_and_radi